6:<p class="status"><b>Numerical qualification and empirical accuracy are separate.</b> Numerical convergence measures changes within the chosen model; it does not establish empirical accuracy. Across selected STA post-calibration hourly pairs, flux Pearson r=-0.09005, median absolute fractional flux error 45%, and speed error 20%. The fixed STA600km/s three-hour crossing is 32 h late in the Nov26–Dec01 comparison. The observed sustained650km/s wind is absent from the selected model in that fixed window. ccmc ambient PN incident flux is 0.3319× the selected estimate. ccmc cme PN incident flux is 0.3322× the selected estimate. Between-model differences and spacecraft residuals constrain interpretation; they are not calibrated confidence bounds.</p>
7:<p class="status">Recorded selected spatial and timestep controls pass their numerical targets. Outstanding evidence: failed physical scenario ct96_w30_cooler has no qualified complete matched response; failed physical scenario ct96_w30_cooler_half has no qualified complete matched response; failed physical scenario ct96_w30_gamma53 has no qualified complete matched response; failed physical scenario ct96_w30_gamma53_half has no qualified complete matched response. Tested input/physics ranges are sensitivity studies, not confidence intervals or exhaustive accuracy bounds.</p>
29:<p>Published pointing: 2025-12-03T12:37:38 to 2025-12-04T17:22:29 UTC. The PN comparison uses 45 exact accepted intervals in 8 frames, totaling 21362.284 s; accepted bounds are 2025-12-03T13:04:03.002 to 2025-12-04T09:56:03.002. The wider published pointing and legacy analysis-frame bounds are distinct from accepted exposure. <a href="observations/xmm_gtis.tsv">Exact detector GTIs</a> · <a href="observations/inputs_summary.json">Epoch evidence</a>.</p>
30:<p><b>Selected curve/GTI clock: Observer-arrival UTC: native comet states and comet velocity are evaluated at emission UTC and relabeled at arrival at the geometric Earth proxy.</b> Native spacecraft checks and movie frames retain physical geometric UTC. Photon delay is not solar-wind travel from Earth to the comet. Geometric Earth is an approximate XMM observer; the orbital light-path difference is below one second. <a href="https://xmm-tools.cosmos.esa.int/external/xmm_user_support/documentation/uhb/orbit.html">ESA orbit information</a>; <a href="validation/observer_clock_qualification.json">Clock implementation qualification</a>.</p>
